<Project>

  <ItemGroup Label="IPC">
    <PackageVersion Include="StreamJsonRpc" Version="2.21.10" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Nerdbank.Streams" Version="2.11.79" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="IPC transitive vulnerability pins">
    <!-- CORRECTION (not suppression) for GHSA-hv8m-jj95-wg3x: MessagePack
         2.5.187 has a CVSS 8.2 high-severity LZ4 decompression OOB read
         vulnerability. StreamJsonRpc 2.21.10's .nuspec declares
         "MessagePack >= 2.5.187" unconditionally for its optional
         MessagePack formatter path; we don't construct that formatter
         (our wire is HeaderDelimitedMessageHandler JSON via
         ZenVizorRpcHost.Host / ZenVizorRpcClient.Attach) and don't import
         a single MessagePack API anywhere in the codebase, so the
         vulnerable code path (Lz4Block / Lz4BlockArray decompression) is
         unreachable today — but the package version in the graph still
         carries the patch obligation.

         Pin to MessagePack 2.5.301 (the patched 2.x release per the
         advisory) via Central Package Management + transitive pinning
         (CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled in Directory.Build.props).
         Stays on the same minor as StreamJsonRpc's floor — ABI-compatible
         with the assembly StreamJsonRpc loads at runtime. v3.x is not used
         because it crosses a major boundary and the LZ4 codec moved to a
         separate package; staying on 2.5.x is the conservative diff.

         MessagePack.Annotations is pinned in lockstep — same release train,
         compile-time-only attribute carriers, but kept aligned so a future
         analyzer query against the assets graph reports a single coherent
         MessagePack version. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="MessagePack" Version="2.5.301" />
    <PackageVersion Include="MessagePack.Annotations" Version="2.5.301" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Storage">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="10.0.9" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Storage transitive vulnerability pins">
    <!-- CORRECTION (not suppression) for GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q (CVE-2025-6965):
         the deprecated SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3 2.1.11 bundles a pre-3.50.2
         SQLite binary with a CVSS 7.2 high-severity aggregate-query memory
         corruption flaw. Microsoft.Data.Sqlite 10.0.0's transitive graph
         resolves to that vulnerable package by default because its
         declared constraint on SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3 is ">= 2.1.11".
         The SQLitePCLRaw maintainer migrated the native binary to a new
         package family (SourceGear.sqlite3) in the 3.0 line and stopped
         shipping the .lib.e_sqlite3 package altogether — there will be no
         2.1.12 fix.

         Pin SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3 and SQLitePCLRaw.core to 3.0.3
         via Central Package Management. The bundle 3.0.3 depends on
         SourceGear.sqlite3 >= 3.50.4.5 (carrying SQLite 3.50.4, past the
         advisory's 3.50.2 fix line) and no longer depends on the
         deprecated SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3. With
         CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled (Directory.Build.props) the
         pin overrides Microsoft.Data.Sqlite 10.0.0's transitive floor;
         semver-wise the 3.0.x pin satisfies the >= 2.1.11 constraint, so
         the Microsoft.Data.Sqlite.Core API surface stays bound to the same
         resolver pattern (the bundle pattern is intentionally swappable —
         that's the whole reason SQLitePCLRaw splits core/lib/bundle).

         REACHABILITY (recorded for the next maintainer): the vulnerable
         code path is aggregate query handling (SUM, COUNT, GROUP BY etc.)
         which AppHistoryQueryRepository uses heavily. The trigger
         conditions per the advisory require an attacker capable of either
         (a) writing crafted SQL or (b) writing crafted DB content. Neither
         is reachable in ZenVizor's threat model: the IPC surface uses
         parameterized queries with input validation, the DB is owned
         exclusively by the service (CLAUDE.md invariant #3), and ProgramData
         is ACL'd to SYSTEM + Administrators. Fixing it anyway because the
         pin is cheap and "unreachable today" can flip to "reachable
         tomorrow" with one careless query change. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3" Version="3.0.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SQLitePCLRaw.core" Version="3.0.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Capture / Attribution (Win32)">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Diagnostics.Tracing.TraceEvent" Version="3.2.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Hosting / Logging">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.WindowsServices" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging" Version="10.0.9" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.EventLog" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog" Version="4.2.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.Extensions.Hosting" Version="9.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.Sinks.File" Version="6.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.Sinks.EventLog" Version="4.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="UI">
    <PackageVersion Include="WPF-UI" Version="4.0.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="H.NotifyIcon.Wpf" Version="2.4.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="LiveChartsCore.SkiaSharpView.WPF" Version="2.0.5" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="CLI">
    <PackageVersion Include="System.CommandLine" Version="2.0.0-beta4.22272.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.4" />
    <PackageVersion Include="FluentAssertions" Version="6.12.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="10.0.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Installer (WiX)">
    <!-- WiX 6.0.x packages (SDK, Util, Bal, NetFx) all ship under the
         Open Source Maintenance Fee Agreement (OSMFEULA.txt in each
         package). The source code remains under the OSI-approved
         Microsoft Reciprocal License; the OSMF is a maintenance fee on
         the binary releases that applies ONLY to revenue-generating
         use of the software (Section 1 of the EULA). ZenVizor's current
         non-revenue-generating use is exempt — no fee owed. If ZenVizor
         ever becomes a revenue-generating product, the OSMF applies to
         every WiX 6.0.x package pinned here, not just one of them, and
         the licensing posture should be re-reviewed against the
         then-current EULA terms.

         An earlier rev of this file (and docs/zenvizor-sprint-plan.md
         Phase 7) claimed "WiX 6.0.1 is the last MIT-licensed release."
         That was incorrect — the MIT-only line was WiX 5.x. The
         practical effect is the same (non-revenue-generating projects
         pay nothing), but the wording is now accurate.

         The WiX SDK itself is pinned via the
         <Project Sdk="WixToolset.Sdk/6.0.1"> reference in
         installer/ZenVizor.wixproj and installer/Bundle/ZenVizor.Bundle.wixproj. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="WixToolset.Util.wixext" Version="6.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="WixToolset.Bal.wixext" Version="6.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="WixToolset.NetFx.wixext" Version="6.0.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
